<h2>The incident</h2>
<p>On the evening of 9 April 2026, near a petrol and CNG pump on Vinay Marg, a motorcycle struck the left side of a car. The exchange that followed escalated in seconds: the complainant alleged that the motorcyclist abused him and struck him on the head with a helmet, knocking him unconscious. An FIR was registered the next day at PS Chanakyapuri, and the rider was identified through CCTV footage. The medico-legal certificate recorded the injuries as simple; the complainant was discharged from hospital the following day. The accused obtained regular bail from the sessions court within two weeks. What remained was the prosecution itself — under Section 110 BNS, an offence punishable as an attempt to commit culpable homicide and not compoundable.</p>

<h2>The legal route: inherent power, not compounding</h2>
<p>Because Section 110 BNS cannot be compounded, the parties\' settlement had to travel through the High Court\'s inherent jurisdiction under Section 528 of the BNSS, the successor to Section 482 CrPC. The governing principles are those of <em>Gian Singh</em>, <em>Narinder Singh</em> and <em>Ramgopal</em>: the power is of wide amplitude but must be exercised carefully; offences with a predominantly civil or private flavour, arising from personal disputes, may be quashed on genuine settlement, while heinous offences and crimes against society stand apart. The question is always whether, with the complainant genuinely reconciled, the realistic prospect of conviction has become so remote that continuing the trial is an abuse of process.</p>

<h2>Why this case qualified</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Sudden, not premeditated.</strong> A traffic altercation between strangers, with no prior enmity — the paradigm of a private quarrel rather than a crime against society.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Simple injuries.</strong> The MLC opined the injuries were simple and the complainant was discharged the next day.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Clean antecedents.</strong> The petitioner had no criminal record.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>A verified settlement.</strong> The complainant filed an affidavit-cum-NOC and personally confirmed in court that the settlement was voluntary and he no longer wished to prosecute.</div>
</div>

<p>On that matrix the Court held that the prospect of conviction had become exceedingly remote and that continuation of the proceedings would serve no useful purpose. The FIR and all consequential proceedings were quashed, subject to Rs 10,000 costs payable to the Delhi High Court Legal Services Committee — with the Court careful, as always, to record no conclusive finding on the merits.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>Practitioners should note what the judgment does not do: it does not suggest that every Section 110 BNS case settles its way out of court. The provision covers conduct up to the brink of culpable homicide, and where injuries are grievous, weapons are carried to the scene, or the attack is premeditated, Delhi courts routinely refuse settlement-based quashing. The private-quarrel character of the incident is the gateway fact.</p>
</div>

<h2>The practical sequence for settling parties</h2>
<p>For parties who have genuinely reconciled after an incident of this kind, the judgment maps the standard Delhi sequence: reduce the settlement to writing; obtain the complainant\'s affidavit supporting quashing; file a petition under Section 528 BNSS impleading both the State and the complainant; produce the complainant for the court\'s interaction; and be prepared for costs as a condition. Timing matters less than voluntariness — settlements reached before or after chargesheet can both sustain quashing — but the court\'s satisfaction that no pressure operated on the injured party is non-negotiable, and a complainant who wavers in court will usually end the petition.</p>
